#!/bin/bash
# post-commit hook — DISABLED (see issue #627)
#
# This hook used to increment .version and amend the commit. It cannot work
# under the current PR-only workflow, and had been silently doing nothing for
# some time: `main` sat at 1.5.0 across several `feat:` merges that each should
# have bumped MINOR.
#
# Why it is unreachable, in both directions:
#
#   1. It exited early unless the current branch was `main`. Every commit is
#      now authored on a fix/* or dev/* branch, so that check exited 0.
#   2. The commit that actually lands on `main` is created by GitHub's
#      squash-merge, server-side, where no local hook runs at all.
#
# So there was no point at which the old logic could fire. Amending is also
# wrong for this workflow independently: it would rewrite a commit that has
# already been pushed to `main`.
#
# Deliberately kept as an explanatory no-op rather than deleted, so the reason
# is discoverable from the path the docs point at, and so `make install-hooks`
# (which chmods this file) keeps working.
#
# A replacement needs to run on push-to-main in CI and parse the squashed PR
# title for the conventional-commit type. It must also keep .version, the
# OpenAPI info.version, and the server build version in step — they are
# currently independent and have already drifted. See #627.
#
# scripts/update-version.sh is left in place: it still works when invoked
# directly, and whatever replaces this hook will likely reuse it.

exit 0
